 "minutes": 40,
 "blocks": [
  {
   "id": "intro",
   "type": "intro",
   "heading": {
    "en": "Two switches, one bulb"
   },
   "hook": {
    "en": "A corridor light comes on only if both the main switch and the timer are on. A doorbell rings if either of two buttons is pressed. Circuits like these follow the same logic as \"and\" and \"or\"."
   },
   "objectives": [
    {
     "en": "Form compound statements with \"and\" and \"or\""
    },
    {
     "en": "Find their truth values"
    },
    {
     "en": "Use inclusive and exclusive \"or\" and De Morgan's laws"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A corridor light comes on only if both the main switch and the timer are on. A doorbell rings if either of two buttons is pressed. Circuits like these follow the same logic as \"and\" and \"or\"."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "concept1",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "And, or, and their truth tables"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "A compound statement is built from simple statements with connectives. p ∧ q (\"p and q\") is true only if both p and q are true. p ∨ q (\"p or q\", inclusive) is true if at least one is true; it is false only when both are false. Exclusive or is true when exactly one is true. De Morgan's laws: $\\sim(p \\wedge q) \\equiv \\sim p \\vee \\sim q$ and $\\sim(p \\vee q) \\equiv \\sim p \\wedge \\sim q$."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "p ∧ q: both true"
    },
    {
     "en": "p ∨ q: at least one true"
    },
    {
     "en": "not(and) = or of nots; not(or) = and of nots"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A compound statement is built from simple statements with connectives. p ∧ q (\"p and q\") is true only if both p and q are true. p ∨ q (\"p or q\", inclusive) is true if at least one is true; it is false only when both are false. Exclusive or is true when exactly one is true. De Morgan's laws: $\\sim(p \\wedge q) \\equiv \\sim p \\vee \\sim q$ and $\\sim(p \\vee q) \\equiv \\sim p \\wedge \\sim q$."
   },
   "formula": "\\sim(p \\wedge q) \\equiv \\sim p \\vee \\sim q"
  },
